Yes, tolls and parking fees for business trips are deducted on top of the 2026 IRS mileage rate, but fuel, repairs, tyres, insurance and depreciation are not. [IRS Topic 510](https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc510) lists parking fees and tolls tied to business use as separately deductible, whether you use the standard mileage rate or actual expenses. The 2026 rates of 72.5 cents per mile (January to June) and 76 cents (July to December) already cover gas and the car's ownership costs, so you never claim those a second time.

If you are self-employed, both amounts go on Schedule C. In the example on this page, 12,000 business miles at a blended 74.25 cents comes to $8,910, and your toll and parking total is added on top of that $8,910. Keep toll statements and parking receipts with the mileage log that records each trip's date, destination and business purpose.